\clearpage
\section*{144.}
\subsection*{A THIRD MEMOIR UPON QUANTICS.}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{144. A Third Memoir Upon Quantics}

[From the \textit{Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London}, vol.~cxlvi.\ for the
year 1856, pp.~627---647. Received March 13,---Read April 10, 1856.]

\bigskip

My object in the present memoir is chiefly to collect together and put upon
record various results useful in the theories of the particular quantics to which
they relate. The tables at the commencement relate to binary quantics, and are a
direct sequel to the tables in my Second Memoir upon Quantics, vol.~cxlvi.~(1856), [141].
The definitions and explanations in the next part of the present memoir are given
here for the sake of convenience, the further development of the subjects to which
they relate being reserved for another occasion. The remainder of the memoir consists
of tables and explanations relating to ternary quadrics and cubics.

\bigskip
\noindent\textsc{Covariant and other Tables, Nos.~27 to 50 (Nos.~1 to 50 binary quantics).}\footnote{The Tables 49 and 50 were inserted October 6, 1856.---A.~C.}

Nos.~27 to 29 are a continuation of the tables relating to the quintic
\[(a, b, c, d, e, f \, \wr\, x, y)^5.\]

No.~27 gives the values of the different determinants of the matrix
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
a, & 4b, & 6c, & 4d, & e \\
a, & 4b, & 6c, & 4d, & e \\
  & b, & 4c, & 6d, & 4e, & f \\
  & b, & 4c, & 6d, & 4e, & f
\end{vmatrix}
\]
determinants which are represented by 1234, 1235, \&c., where the numbers refer to
the different columns of the matrix. No.~28 gives the values of certain linear
functions of these determinants, viz.
\begin{align*}
L &= \phantom{3\cdot}1256 + \phantom{2\cdot}2345 - 2\cdot 1346,\\
L' &= 3\cdot 1256 - \phantom{3\cdot}1346,\\
8M &= -1345 + 2\cdot 1246,\\
8M' &= -2346 + 2\cdot 1356,\\
8N &= -1245 + 3\cdot 1236,\\
8N' &= -2356 + 3\cdot 1456,\\
80P &= \phantom{-5}L' - 3L = \phantom{-}5\cdot 1346 - 3\cdot 2345,\\
16P' &= -5L' - \phantom{3}L = -16\cdot 1256 - 3\cdot 1346 - 2356.
\end{align*}

At the end of the two tables there are given certain relations which exist between
the terms of Tables 14, 16, 25, 26, 27 and 28.

\clearpage
We have also the following relations between $L$, $L'$, \&c.\ and $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, $e$, $f$,
viz.
\begin{align*}
aP - bM + cN &= 0,\\
aM' + bP' - 2cM + 3dN &= 0,\\
aN' + 2bM' - cL' + 3eN &= 0,\\
3bN' - dL' + 2eM + fN &= 0,\\
3cN' - 2dM' + eP' + fM &= 0,\\
dN' - eM' + fP &= 0.
\end{align*}

The quartinvariant No.~19 [$G$] is equal to $-AC + B^2$,
i.e.\ it is in fact equal to $-4$ into the discriminant of the quintic No.~14, [$A$].

The octinvariant No.~25 [$Q$] is expressible in terms of the coefficients of Nos.~14 and 16,
viz.\ $A$, $B$, $C$, as before, and $\alpha$, $\beta$, $\gamma$, $\delta$ the coefficients of No.~16, [$D$],
i.e.
\begin{align*}
\alpha &= 3(ace - ad^2 - b^2e + 2bcd - c^3),\\
\beta &= acf - ade - b^2f + bd^2 + bce - cd^2,\\
\gamma &= adf - ae^2 - bcf + bde + c^2e - cd^2,\\
\delta &= 3(bdf - be^2 + 2cde - c^2f - d^3),
\end{align*}
then No.~25 is equal to
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
A, & B, & C \\
\alpha, & \beta, & \gamma \\
\beta, & \gamma, & \delta
\end{vmatrix}
.\]

The value of the discriminant No.~26, [$Q'$], is
\[\text{(No.~19)}^2 - 128\,\text{No.~25},\]
[that is $Q' = G^2 - 128Q$.]

We have also an expression for the discriminant in terms of $L$, $L'$, \&c., viz.
three times the discriminant No.~26 is equal to
\[[\text{or say }3Q' =]\, LL' + 64MM' - 64NN',\]
a remarkable formula, the discovery of which is due to Mr~Salmon.

It may be noticed, that in the particular case in which the quintic has two square
factors, if we write
\[(a, b, c, d, e, f \wr x, y)^5 = \{(p, q, r \wr x, y)^2\}^2 \cdot (\lambda, \mu \wr x, y),\]
\clearpage
The table No.~29 is the invariant of the twelfth degree of the quintic,
given in its simplest form, i.e.\ in a form not containing any power higher than
the fourth of the leading coefficient $a$: this invariant was first calculated by M.~Faa de Bruno.

\noindent\textsc{No.~29.} [See U.\ No.~29, p.~294.]

The tables Nos.~30 to 35 relate to a \textbf{sextic}.
No.~30 is the sextic itself; No.~31 the quadrinvariant; Nos.~32 and 33 the quadricovariants
(the latter of them the Hessian); No.~34 is the quartinvariant or catalecticant;
and No.~35 is the sextinvariant in its best form, i.e.\ a form not containing any
power higher than the second of the leading coefficient $a$.

\noindent\textsc{No.~30.}
\[(a, b, c, d, e, f, g \, \wr\, x, y)^6.\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~34.}
\begin{align*}
&\pm 12 a^2d^2g^2 + 1\cdot ace^2g - 42\cdot bcdefg + 60\cdot a^2defg - 6\cdot ace^3f + 12\cdot bce^2f^2 - 30\cdot abef^3\\
&\quad + 4\cdot ad^4g - 20\cdot bcd^2eg + 24\cdot a^2e^2g - 3\cdot ad^3e^2 - 8\cdot bc^2d^2f + 66\cdot abcdfg - 6\cdot abcdg^2\\
&\quad + 4\cdot b^2c^2ef - 84\cdot abc^2eg + 18\cdot abce^2f + 24\cdot b^2cde^2 - 12\cdot b^3e^2g + 8\cdot c^4dg + 12\cdot ab^2d^2f\\
&\quad - 27\cdot c^3e^3 + 30\cdot c^3d^2g - 8\cdot a^3e^3 + 6\cdot b^2ce^2f - 24\cdot c^2e^2f + 66\cdot ac^2d^2e - 4\cdot b^3c^2g\\
&\quad - 24\cdot b^2c^2d^2 - 15\cdot a^2c^2f^2 + 60\cdot b^2c^2ef - 12\cdot c^4e^2 - 8\cdot ac^3df + 36\cdot a^2cd^2f + 54\cdot acd^3e\\
&\quad + 6\cdot b^2c^3f \pm 565
\end{align*}

The sextinvariant may be thus represented by means of a determinant of the
sixth order and of the quadrinvariant and quartinvariant.
\[5 \times \text{No.~35} =
\begin{vmatrix}
a, & 2b, & 3c, & 4d, & e \\
b, & 2c, & 3d, & 4e, & f \\
c, & 2d, & 3e, & 4f, & g \\
a, & 4b, & 3c, & 2d, & e \\
b, & 4c, & 3d, & 2e, & f \\
c, & 4d, & 3e, & 2f, & g
\end{vmatrix}
+ 4(ag - 6bf + 15ce - 10d^2)
\begin{vmatrix}
a, & b, & c, & d \\
b, & c, & d, & e \\
c, & d, & e, & f \\
d, & e, & f, & g
\end{vmatrix}.\]

\clearpage
\noindent\textbf{Definitions.}

The quantic or quantics to which it belongs, and with indeterminate coefficients
$(\xi, \eta, \ldots)$. The invariants of a quantic or quantics, and of an adjoint linear form, may
be considered as quantics having $(\xi, \eta, \ldots)$ for facients, and of which the coefficients
are of course functions of the coefficients of the given quantic or quantics.
An invariant of the class in question is termed a \textit{contravariant} of the quantic
or quantics. The idea of a contravariant is due to Mr Sylvester.

In the theory of binary quantics, it is hardly necessary to consider the contra-
variants; for any contravariant is at once turned into an invariant by writing
$(y, -x)$ for $(\xi, \eta)$.

\textbf{57.} If we imagine, as before, a system of quantics of the form
\[(\bullet\, \wr\, x, y, \ldots)^n,\]
where the number of qua

\bigskip
The coefficients of this quantic are termed \textit{Emanants}, viz., excluding the first
coefficient, which is the quantic itself (but which might be termed the 0-th emanant),
the other coefficients are the first, second, and last or ultimate emanants.

The ultimate emanant is, it is clear, nothing else than the quantic itself,
with $(X, Y, \ldots)$ instead of $(x, y, \ldots)$ for facients: the penultimate emanant is, in like
manner, obtained from the first emanant by interchanging $(x, y, \ldots)$ with $(X, Y, \ldots)$,
and similarly for the other emanants. The facients $(X, Y, \ldots)$ may be termed
the facients of emanation, or simply the new facients.

Such covariants are called \textit{Combinants}, and they possess the property of being
invariantive, quoad the system, i.e.\ the covariant remains unaltered to a factor pr\`es,
when each quantic is replaced by a linear function of all the quantics.
This extremely important theory is due to Mr Sylvester.

\clearpage
\noindent\textsc{Covariant and other Tables, Nos.~51 to 56 (a ternary quadric).}

\noindent\textsc{No.~51.} The quadric is represented by $(a, b, c, f, g, h \, \wr\, x, y, z)^2$,
which means
\[ax^2 + by^2 + cz^2 + 2fyz + 2gzx + 2hxy.\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~52.} The first derived functions (omitting the factor 2) are
\[(a, h, g \, \wr\, x, y, z),\quad (h, b, f \, \wr\, x, y, z),\quad (g, f, c \, \wr\, x, y, z).\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~53.} The operators which reduce a covariant to zero are
\begin{align*}
&(j, 3f, c, 2i, g, 2h \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - y\,\partial_z,\\
&(a, k, 3f, 2i, 2j, h \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - z\,\partial_x,\\
&(3h, b, i, f, 2i, 2k \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - x\,\partial_y,\\
&\text{\&c.}
\end{align*}

\noindent\textsc{No.~55.} The discriminant is
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
a, & h, & g \\
h, & b, & f \\
g, & f, & c
\end{vmatrix}
\]
which is equal to
\[abc - af^2 - bg^2 - ch^2 + 2fgh.\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~56.} The reciprocant is
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
\xi, & \eta, & \zeta \\
\xi, & h, & g \\
\eta, & b, & f \\
\zeta, & f, & c
\end{vmatrix}^2
\]
which is equal to
\[(bc-f^2,\ ca-g^2,\ ab-h^2,\ gh-af,\ hf-bg,\ fg-ch \, \wr\, \xi, \eta, \zeta)^2.\]
The discriminant is, it will be noticed, the same function as the Hessian.
The reciprocant is the evectant of the discriminant.
The covariants are the quadric itself and the discriminant; the reciprocant is the only contravariant.

\clearpage
\noindent\textsc{Covariant and other Tables, Nos.~57 to 70 (a ternary cubic).}

\noindent\textsc{No.~57.} The cubic is $U = (a, b, c, f, g, h, i, j, k, l \, \wr\, x, y, z)^3$,
which means
\[ax^3 + by^3 + cz^3 + 3fyz^2 + 3gz^2x + 3hx^2y + 3iy^2z + 3jzx^2 + 3kxy^2 + 6lxyz.\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~58.} The first derived functions (omitting the factor 3) are
\begin{align*}
&(a, h, j, l \, \wr\, x, y, z)^2,\\
&(l, b, f, i \, \wr\, x, y, z)^2,\\
&(g, l, c, f \, \wr\, x, y, z)^2,\\
&(h, k, l, g \, \wr\, x, y, z)^2.
\end{align*}

\noindent\textsc{No.~59.} The operators which reduce a covariant to zero are
\begin{align*}
&(j, 3f, c, 2i, g, 2l \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - y\,\partial_z,\\
&(a, k, 3h, 2l, 2j, g \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - z\,\partial_x,\\
&(3h, b, i, f, 2l, 2k \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - x\,\partial_y,\\
&(3j, f, i, c, 2g, 2l \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - z\,\partial_x,\\
&(a, l, g, 2j, h, 2l \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - x\,\partial_z,\\
&(2l, b, 3i, f, k, 2h \, \wr\, \partial_x, \partial_y, \partial_z)^3 - y\,\partial_x.\\
\end{align*}

\noindent\textsc{No.~60.} The evector is
\[(3a, 3b, 3c, 3f, 3g, 3h, 3i, 3j, 3k, 3l \, \wr\, \xi, \eta, \zeta)^3.\]

\noindent\textsc{No.~61.} The Hessian is $HU =$
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
(a, h, j, l \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (h, k, l, g \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (j, l, g, i \, \wr\, x, y, z) \\
(h, k, l, g \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (k, b, f, i \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (l, f, i, c \, \wr\, x, y, z) \\
(j, l, g, i \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (l, f, i, c \, \wr\, x, y, z), & (g, i, c, f \, \wr\, x, y, z)
\end{vmatrix}
\]
or in the developed form
\begin{align*}
&a^2bcd + \ldots \\
\Theta_3 U &= -2(1 + 3l^2)(yz^2 + z^2x + x^2y) + (1 - 9l^2)(x^3 + y^3 + z^3)\\
&\quad + (3l - 9l^2 + 96l^3)(x^2 + y^2 + z^2)xyz + \ldots
\end{align*}

\clearpage
\noindent\textsc{No.~62.} The quartinvariant is $\Omega =$
\begin{align*}
&\phantom{+} abcl - 1\cdot fgh + 1\cdot abgi + 1\cdot f^2hk + 3\cdot c^2fjk + 1\cdot fgjk - 1\cdot a^2i^2 - 1\cdot h^2ij - 1\cdot afil + 1\cdot fjl^2\\
& - 2\cdot a^2k^2 + 1\cdot c^2h^2k + 1\cdot bchj + 1\cdot ghki - 1\cdot bf^2h - 2\cdot g^2h^2 - 2\cdot bgji + 1\cdot h^3l + 1\cdot c^2j^2\\
& - 1\cdot irj^2 - 1\cdot cf^2i + 3\cdot c^2h^2k + 1\cdot l^4 + 1\cdot cjk^2 \pm 16
\end{align*}

\noindent\textsc{No.~63.} The sextinvariant is $\Sigma =$
\begin{align*}
