\clearpage
\section*{145.}
\subsection*{A MEMOIR UPON CAUSTICS.}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{145. A Memoir Upon Caustics}

[From the \textit{Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London}, vol.~cxlvii.\ for the
year 1857, pp.~273---312. Received May 1,---Read May 8, 1856.]

\bigskip

The following memoir contains little or nothing that can be considered new in
principle; the object of it is to collect together the principal results relating to
caustics in plano, the reflecting or refracting curve being a right line or a circle,
and to discuss, with more care than appears to have been hitherto bestowed upon
the subject, some of the more remarkable cases.

The memoir contains in particular researches relating to the caustic by refraction
of a circle for parallel rays, the caustic by reflexion of a circle for rays proceeding
from a point, and the caustic by refraction of a circle for rays proceeding from a
point; the result in the last case is not worked out, but it is shown how the
equation in rectangular coordinates is to be obtained by equating to zero the
discriminant of a rational and integral function of the sixth degree.

The memoir treats also of the secondary caustic, or orthogonal trajectory of the
reflected or refracted rays, in the general case of a reflecting or refracting circle
and rays proceeding from a point; the curve in question, or rather a secondary
caustic, is, as is well known, the Oval of Descartes or `Cartesian': the equation is
discussed by a method which gives rise to some forms of the curve which appear
to have escaped the notice of geometers.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{I.}

Consider a ray of light reflected or refracted at a curve, and suppose that
$\xi$, $\eta$ are the coordinates of a point Q on the incident ray, $\alpha$, $\beta$ the coordinates
of the point G of incidence upon the reflecting or refracting curve, $a$, $b$ the
coordinates of a point N upon the normal at the point of incidence, $x$, $y$ the
coordinates of a point $q$ on the reflected or refracted ray. Write for shortness,
\begin{align*}
(b-\beta)(\xi-\alpha) - (a-\alpha)(\eta-\beta) &= \nabla\,QGN,\\
(a-\alpha)(\xi-\alpha) + (b-\beta)(\eta-\beta) &= \triangle\,QGN,
\end{align*}
then $\nabla\,QGN$ is equal to twice the area of the triangle $QGN$, and if $\xi$,
$\eta$ instead of being the coordinates of a point Q on the incident ray were current
coordinates, the equation $\nabla\,QGN = 0$ would be the equation of the line through
the points G and N, i.e.\ of the normal at the point of incidence; and in like
manner the equation $\triangle\,QGN = 0$ would be the equation of the line through G
perpendicular to the line through the points G and N, i.e.\ of the tangent at the
point of incidence. We have identically,
\[NG = (a-\alpha)^2 + (b-\beta)^2,\]
\[NG \cdot QG = \nabla\,QGN + \triangle\,QGN.\]

Suppose for a moment that $\phi$ is the angle of incidence and $\phi'$ the angle of
reflexion or refraction; and let $\mu$ be the index of refraction, then
\[\sin\phi' = \mu\sin\phi.\]

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{II.}

But instead of investigating the nature of the caustic itself, we may begin by
finding the secondary caustic or orthogonal trajectory of the refracted rays, i.e.\ a
curve having the caustic for its evolute; suppose that the incident rays are all of
them normal to a certain curve, and let Q be a point upon this curve, and
considering the ray through the point Q, let G be the point of incidence upon the
refracting curve; then if the point G be made the centre of a circle the radius of
which is $\mu^{-1}\cdot GQ$, the envelope of the circles will be the secondary caustic.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{IV.}

If rays proceeding from a point Q are reflected at a line, the reflected rays will
proceed from a point $q$ situate on the perpendicular let fall from Q, and at an equal
distance on the other side of the reflecting line. The point $q$ may be spoken of as
the image of Q; it is clear that if Q be considered as a variable point, then the
locus of the image $q$ will be a curve equal and similar but oppositely situated to
the curve, the locus of Q, and which may be spoken of as the image of such curve.
Hence it at once follows, that if the incidental rays are tangent, or normal, or indeed
in any other manner related to a curve, then the reflected rays will be tangent, or
normal, or related in a corresponding manner to a curve the image of the first-mentioned curve.

\clearpage
\noindent\textbf{V.}

The incident rays are here parallel rays, and the refracting curve a right line.
We have $\sin\phi' = k\sin\phi$, and the equation $y - x\tan\phi' = \tan\phi$ of the refracted
ray becomes, putting for $\phi'$ its value,
\[y - \frac{k\sin\phi}{\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}}x - \tan\phi = 0.\]
Differentiating with respect to the variable parameter and combining the two
equations, we obtain, after a simple reduction,
\[kx = -\frac{k^2y - k'^2\sin^2\phi}{\cos^2\phi},\]
where $k' = \sqrt{1-k^2}$; hence eliminating,
\[(kx)^2 - (k'y)^2 = k^2k'^2,\]
which is the equation of the caustic. When the refraction takes place into a
denser medium $k$ is less than 1, and $k'^2$ is positive, the caustic is therefore the
evolute of a hyperbola (see fig.~1); but when the refraction takes place in a
rarer medium $k$ is greater than 1, and $k'^2$ is negative, the caustic is therefore the
evolute of an ellipse (see fig.~2). These results appear to have been first obtained
by Gergonne.

The conic (hyperbola or ellipse) is the secondary caustic, and as such may be
obtained as follows.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{VI.}

The equation of the variable circle is
\[x^2 + (y - \tan\phi)^2 - k^2\sec^2\phi = 0;\]
or reducing, the equation is
\[x^2 + y^2 - 2y\tan\phi + k'^2\tan^2\phi - k^2 = 0:\]
whence, considering $\tan\phi$ as the variable parameter, the equation of the envelope is
\[k'^2(x^2+y^2-k^2) - y^2 = 0,\]
that is,
\[k'^2x^2 - k^2y^2 - k^2k'^2 = 0,\]
is the equation of the secondary caustic, or conic having the caustic for its evolute.
The radiant point, it is clear, is a focus of the conic.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{VII.}

Let the equation of the refracted ray be represented by
\[Xx + Yy + Z = 0,\]
we have
\[X : Y : Z = -\frac{k^2\sin\phi}{\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}} : 1 : -\tan\phi,\]
from which we obtain
\[\frac{\xi^2}{X^2} + \frac{\eta^2}{Y^2} = \frac{1}{k^2}\]
for the tangential equation of the caustic; or if we represent the equation of the
refracted ray by
\[Xx + Yy - k = 0,\]
then we have
\[\frac{\xi^2}{X^2} + \frac{\eta^2}{Y^2} = \frac{1}{k^2}\]
for the tangential equation of the caustic.

\clearpage
\noindent\textbf{VIII.}

If a ray be reflected at a circle; we may take $a$, $b$ as the coordinates of
the centre of the circle, and supposing as before that $\xi$, $\eta$ are the coordinates of
a point Q in the incident ray, $\alpha$, $\beta$ the coordinates of the point G of incidence,
and $x$, $y$ the coordinates of a point $q$ in the reflected ray, the equation of the
reflected ray, treating $x$, $y$ as current coordinates, is
\begin{multline*}
\{(b-\beta)(x-\alpha) - (a-\alpha)(y-\beta)\}\{(a-\alpha)(\xi-\alpha) + (b-\beta)(\eta-\beta)\}\\
+ \{(a-\alpha)(x-\alpha) + (b-\beta)(y-\beta)\}\{(b-\beta)(\xi-\alpha) - (a-\alpha)(\eta-\beta)\} = 0.
\end{multline*}
Write for shortness,
\begin{align*}
\nabla_{q,0} &= (b-\beta)(x-\alpha) - (a-\alpha)(y-\beta),\\
\triangle_{q,0} &= (a-\alpha)(x-\alpha) + (b-\beta)(y-\beta),
\end{align*}
and similarly for $\nabla_{q,0}'$ \&c.; the equation of the reflected ray is
\[\nabla_{q,0'}\triangle_{Q,G} + \triangle_{q,0}\nabla_{Q,G} = 0.\]

Suppose that the reflected ray meets the circle again in $G'$ and undergoes a
second reflexion, and let $x'$, $y'$ be the coordinates of a point $q$ in the ray thus
twice reflected. We see first ($G'$ being a point in the first reflected ray) that
\[\nabla_{q',G'}\triangle_{Q,G} + \triangle_{q',G'}\nabla_{Q,G} = 0.\]

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{IX.}

Consider a ray reflected any number of times at a circle; and let $\theta_0G$
be the ray incident at $G$, and $\theta G'$ the last reflected ray, the point at which
the reflexion takes place or last point of incidence being $G$. Take the centre of
the circle for the origin, and any two lines $Ox$, $Oy$ through the centre and at
right angles to each other for axes, and let $Ox$ meet the circle in the point A.
Write
\[\angle AOG = \theta_0,\quad \angle xG_0G = \psi_0,\quad \angle AOG = \theta,\quad \angle xGG' = \psi,\quad \angle G_0GG' = \phi;\]
then the radius of the circle being taken as the centre of the circle, the equation
of the reflected ray is
\[y - \sin\theta = \tan\psi'(x - \cos\theta);\]
and if there have been $n$ reflexions, then
\[\psi' = \psi_0 + n(\pi - 2\phi) = \psi_0 + n\pi - 2n\phi,\]
\[\psi = \psi_0 - 2n\phi,\]
and therefore the equation of the reflected ray is
\[y\cos(\psi_0 - 2n\phi) - x\sin(\psi_0 - 2n\phi) + (-1)^n\sin(\psi_0 - \theta_0) = 0.\]

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{X.}

If a pencil of parallel rays is reflected any number of times at a circle, then
taking A for the direction of the incident rays, we may write $\theta_0 = \phi$,
$\psi_0 = \pi$, and the equation of a reflected ray is
\[x\sin 2n\phi + y\cos 2n\phi = (-1)^n\sin\phi;\]
differentiating with respect to the variable parameter, we find
\[x\cos 2n\phi - y\sin 2n\phi = (-1)^n\tfrac{1}{2}\cos\phi;\]
and these equations give
\[x = \tfrac{1}{2}\{(2n+1)\cos(2n-1)\phi - (2n-1)\cos(2n+1)\phi\},\]
\[y = (-1)^n\{(2n+1)\sin(2n-1)\phi + (2n-1)\sin(2n+1)\phi\},\]
which may be taken for the equation of the caustic; the caustic is therefore an
epicycloid: this is a well-known result.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{XI.}

If rays proceeding from a point upon the circumference are reflected any
number of times at a circle, then taking the point A for the radiant point, we
have $\theta_0 = 0$, $\psi_0 = \pi - \phi$, and the equation of a reflected ray is
\[x\sin(2n+1)\phi + y\cos(2n+1)\phi = (-1)^n\sin\phi;\]
differentiating with respect to the variable parameter, we find
\[x\cos(2n+1)\phi - y\sin(2n+1)\phi = (-1)^n\tfrac{1}{2}\sin\phi;\]
and these equations give
\[x = \tfrac{1}{2}\{(n+1)\cos 2n\phi - n\cos(2n+2)\phi\},\]
\[y = \tfrac{1}{2}\{-(n+1)\sin 2n\phi + n\sin(2n+2)\phi\},\]
which may be taken as the equation of the caustic; the caustic is therefore in this
case also an epicycloid: this is a well-known result.

\clearpage
\noindent\textbf{XII.}

Consider a pencil of parallel rays refracted at a circle; take the radius of
the circle as unity, and let the incident rays be parallel to the axis of $x$, then if
$\phi$, $\phi'$ be the angles of incidence and refraction, and $\mu$ or $k$ be the index of
refraction, so that $\sin\phi' = k\sin\phi$, the coordinates of the point of incidence are
$\cos\phi$, $\sin\phi$, and the equation of the refracted ray is
\[y - \sin\phi = \tan(\phi - \phi')(x - \cos\phi),\]
or
\[y\cos(\phi - \phi') - x\sin(\phi - \phi') = \sin\phi',\]
which may also be written
\[(y\cos\phi - x\sin\phi)\cos\phi' + (y\sin\phi + x\cos\phi - 1)\sin\phi' = 0.\]
Writing $k\sin\phi$, $\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}$ instead of $\sin\phi'$, $\cos\phi'$, and putting for
shortness
\[Y = y\cos\phi - x\sin\phi,\quad X = y\sin\phi + x\cos\phi,\quad
\omega = \frac{k\sin\phi}{\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}},\]
the equation of the refracted ray becomes
\[Y + \omega(X-1) = 0;\]
and differentiating with respect to the variable parameter $\phi$, observing that
\[\frac{dY}{d\phi} = y,\quad \frac{dX}{d\phi} = x,\quad
\frac{d\omega}{d\phi} = \frac{k\cos\phi}{(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)^{3/2}} = \frac{\omega\cot\phi}{1-k^2\sin^2\phi},\]
we have
\[y + \omega\cdot x + \frac{\omega\cot\phi}{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}(X-1) = 0,\]
and the combination of the two equations gives
\[Y = \frac{\omega(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)}{\omega\cot\phi - 1},\qquad
X = \frac{\omega\cot\phi - k^2\sin^2\phi}{\omega\cot\phi - 1};\]
and we have therefore
\[w = Y\cos\phi + X\sin\phi = \frac{\omega\sin\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi) + \omega\cot\phi\sin\phi - k^2\sin^3\phi}{\omega\cot\phi - 1} = k\sin^3\phi,\]
\begin{align*}
x &= X\cos\phi - Y\sin\phi\\
&= \frac{\omega\cot\phi\cos\phi - k^2\sin^2\phi\cos\phi - \omega\sin^2\phi\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi} + \omega\sin\phi\cos\phi}{\omega\cot\phi - 1}\\
&= \frac{k(1-k^2\sin^2\phi) - k^2\sin^2\phi\cos\phi}{k\cos\phi - \sin\phi\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}}.
\end{align*}

\noindent\textbf{XIII.}

Or multiplying the numerator and denominator by $(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)^{1/2}(k\cos\phi + \sin\phi\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi})$, the numerator becomes
\begin{multline*}
(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)\{k\cos\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi) - k^2\sin^2\phi\cos\phi\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}\\
+ k\sin\phi\sqrt{1-k^2\sin^2\phi}(\sin\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi) - k^2\sin^2\phi\cos\phi)\}\\
= k^3\sin^6\phi\cos\phi\{(1-k^2\sin^2\phi) - \sin^2\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)\}\\
+ k\sin^2\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)^{3/2} = k^3\sin^6\phi\cos^3\phi + k\sin^2\phi(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)^{3/2},
\end{multline*}
and the denominator becomes
\[k^2\sin^2\phi\cos^2\phi - (1-k^2\sin^2\phi)\sin^2\phi = -k'^2\sin^2\phi,\]
if $k'^2 = 1-k^2$.

Hence we have for the coordinates of the point of the caustic,
\[k'^2x = -k'^2\cos^3\phi - \frac{k}{k'}(1-k^2\sin^2\phi)^{3/2},\qquad y = k\sin^3\phi;\]
and eliminating $\phi$, we obtain for the equation of the caustic,
\[k'^2x = -k'^2(1-y^2/k^2)^{3/2} - \frac{k}{k'}(1-y^2)^{3/2};\]
or writing $-\mu$ instead of $k$, we find
\[(1-\mu^2)^{3/2}x = (1-\mu^2y^2/k^2)^{3/2} + \mu(1-y^2)^{3/2}\]
for the equation of the caustic by refraction of the circle, for parallel rays.
The equation was first obtained by St Laurent.

The discussion of the preceding equation presents considerable interest. In the
first place to obtain the rational form write
\[\alpha^2 = \beta^2 + \gamma^2,\]
this gives
\[\alpha^4 - 2\alpha^2(\beta^2 + \gamma^2) + (\beta^2 - \gamma^2)^2 = 0,\]
and we have
\[\gamma^2 = \mu^2 - 3\mu^2\beta^2 + 3\mu^4\beta^2 - \mu^6\beta^6,\]
and consequently
\[\alpha^4 - \beta^4 = (1-\mu^2\beta^2)\{1 - 3\mu^2\beta^2 + (1+\mu^2)\beta^4\}.\]
Hence dividing out by the factor $(1-\mu^2\beta^2)$, the equation becomes
\begin{multline*}
(1-\mu^2)^2x^2 - 2(1+\mu^2)x^2\{1 - 3\mu^2y^2 + (1+\mu^2)y^4\}\\
+ 2(1+\mu^2)x^2y^2 + \{1 - 3\mu^2y^2 + (1+\mu^2)y^4\}^2 = 0,
\end{multline*}
or reducing and arranging,
\begin{multline*}
(1-\mu^2)^2x^4 - 2(1+\mu^2)x^2 + 2(1+\mu^2)x^2y^2 + 1 + 2(1+\mu^2)y^2 + (1+\mu^2)^2y^4\\
+ (1 - 2\mu^2y^2 + 9\mu^4y^4)2x^2 - \{6\mu^2(1+\mu^2)x^2 + 6\mu^2 + 6\mu^2(1+\mu^2)y^2\}y^2 = 0,
\end{multline*}
which is of the form
\[A + 3\mu^2By^2 - 6\mu^2Cy^4 = 0;\]
and the rationalized equation is
\[A^2 + 27\mu^4B^2y^4 - 216\mu^4Cy^4 + 54\mu^2ABCy^2 = 0,\]
where the values of $A$, $B$, $C$ may be written
\[B = 4x^2 + 3y^2;\]
the caustic is therefore a curve of the 12th order.

To find where the axis of $x$ meets the curve, we have $y = 0$, $A^2 = 0$, where
\[A_0 = (1-\mu^2)x^2 - 2(1+\mu^2)x + 1,\]
i.e.
\[x = \frac{1+\mu \pm 2\sqrt{\mu}}{1-\mu^2} = \frac{(1\pm\sqrt{\mu})^2}{(1-\mu)(1+\mu)},\]
or there are in all four points, each of them a point of triple intersection.

To find where the line at infinity meets the curve, we have $x = 0$,
\[A'^2 = 0,\]
where
\[A' = (x^2+y^2)\{(1-\mu^2)x^2 + (1+\mu^2)y^2\},\]
i.e.
\[x = \pm iy,\quad x = \pm\sqrt{\frac{1+\mu^2}{1-\mu^2}}iy.\]
\hfill C.~II.~44
\end{document}
